#lemma[
  Let $E | F$ be a finite extension, $L$ an algebraically closed field and $sigma: F -> L$ an embedding. Then $sigma$ extends to an embedding $E -> L$.
] <lem-extend>

#proof[
  We follow the proof of @thm-embed-count, without separability. We proceed by induction on $[E : F]$; for $[E : F] = 1$ there is nothing to prove. Otherwise choose $alpha in E without F$. The polynomial $g = sigma(m_(alpha, F))$ has a root $beta$ in $L$, since $L$ is algebraically closed, and $g(alpha) mapsto g(beta)$ extends $sigma$ to an embedding $psi: F(alpha) -> L$, as in that proof. Since $[E : F(alpha)] < [E : F]$, the induction hypothesis extends $psi$ to $E$.
]

#proposition[
  Let $E | F$ be a finite extension and $Omega$ an algebraically closed field containing $E$. Then $E | F$ is normal if and only if $tau(E) = E$ for every $F$-monomorphism $tau: E -> Omega$.
] <prop-normal-crit>

#proof[
  This is the proof of @thm-normal-C with $CC$ replaced by $Omega$ and @thm-embed-count replaced by @lem-extend, which needs no separability. In $(arrow.l.double)$, the irreducible $f$ splits into linear factors over the algebraically closed field $Omega$, and every root of $f$ in $Omega$ lies in $E$.
]

#theorem[
  A finite extension $E | F$ is normal if and only if $E$ is a splitting field over $F$ of some $f in F[x]$.
] <thm-normal-split>

#proof[
  $(arrow.r.double)$ Let $[E : F] = n$, let $alpha_1, dots, alpha_n in E$ be a basis of $E$ over $F$, and let $f_i in F[x]$ be the minimal polynomial of $alpha_i$. Since $E | F$ is normal and $f_i$ is irreducible with the root $alpha_i in E$, $f_i$ splits over $E$. Hence $f = f_1 dots.c f_n$ splits over $E$. It remains to show that $E$ is generated by the roots of $f$. If $F subset.eq K subset.eq E$ and $f$ splits over $K$, then $alpha_i in K$ for every $i$, and since the $alpha_i$ span $E$, $K = E$.

  $(arrow.l.double)$ Let $E$ be a splitting field of $f in F[x]$, let $alpha_1, dots, alpha_n$ be the roots of $f$ in $E$, so that $E = F(alpha_1, dots, alpha_n)$, and let $Omega$ be an algebraic closure of $E$ (@rem-recall-fields). Let $tau: E -> Omega$ be an $F$-monomorphism. By the computation in the proof of @thm-perm-roots, each $tau(alpha_i)$ is a root of $f$ in $Omega$. Since $f$ is a product of linear factors over $E$, its roots in $Omega$ are $alpha_1, dots, alpha_n$, so $tau(alpha_i) = alpha_j$ for some $j$. Hence $tau(E) = F(tau(alpha_1), dots, tau(alpha_n)) subset.eq E$. As $tau$ is $F$-linear and injective, $dim_F tau(E) = n = dim_F E$, so $tau(E) = E$. By @prop-normal-crit, $E | F$ is normal.
]

#corollary[
  Let $F subset.eq K subset.eq E$ be fields with $[E : F]$ finite. If $E | F$ is normal, then so is $E | K$.
] <cor-normal-top>

#proof[
  By @thm-normal-split, $E$ is a splitting field over $F$ of some $f in F[x]$, so $E = F(alpha_1, dots, alpha_n)$, where $alpha_1, dots, alpha_n$ are the roots of $f$. Since $f in F[x] subset.eq K[x]$ and $F subset.eq K subset.eq E$, also $E = K(alpha_1, dots, alpha_n)$. Hence $E$ is a splitting field of $f$ over $K$, and $E | K$ is normal by @thm-normal-split.
]

= Galois theory

#definition(title: [Galois extension])[
  An algebraic extension $E | F$ is a _Galois extension_ if it is normal and separable.
]

#definition(title: [Galois group])[
  Let $E | F$ be a field extension. The _Galois group_ $Gal(E | F)$ is the set of $F$-automorphisms of $E$. It is a group under composition, since compositions and inverses of $F$-automorphisms are $F$-automorphisms.
]

#proposition[
  Let $E | F$ be a finite Galois extension with $[E : F] = n$. Then $|Gal(E | F)| = n$.
] <prop-gal-order>

#proof[
  Let $Omega$ be an algebraic closure of $E$ and $iota: E -> Omega$ the inclusion. By @thm-embed-count, applied to the inclusion $F -> Omega$, there are exactly $n$ $F$-monomorphisms $E -> Omega$. Since $E | F$ is normal, each of them has image $E$ by @prop-normal-crit, so it equals $iota compose sigma$ for a unique $sigma in Gal(E | F)$. Conversely $iota compose sigma$ is an $F$-monomorphism for every $sigma in Gal(E | F)$. Hence $sigma mapsto iota compose sigma$ is a bijection from $Gal(E | F)$ onto the $F$-monomorphisms $E -> Omega$.
]

#example[
  $Gal(QQ(root(3, 2)) | QQ) = {"id"}$. Let $sigma$ be a $QQ$-automorphism of $E = QQ(root(3, 2))$. By @thm-perm-roots, $sigma(root(3, 2))$ is a root of $x^3 - 2$ in $E subset.eq RR$. Since $t mapsto t^3$ is strictly increasing on $RR$, the only real root is $root(3, 2)$, so $sigma(root(3, 2)) = root(3, 2)$, and $sigma$ is the identity on $QQ(root(3, 2))$. Here $|Gal(E | QQ)| = 1 < 3 = [E : QQ]$; @prop-gal-order does not apply, since $E | QQ$ is not normal (@ex-normal).
]

#definition(title: [Fixed field])[
  Let $E | F$ be an extension, $G = Gal(E | F)$ and $H <= G$ a subgroup. The _fixed field_ of $H$ is
  $ cal(F)(H) = {x in E : sigma(x) = x "for all" sigma in H}. $
  It is a subfield of $E$, since each $sigma in H$ is a ring homomorphism, and it contains $F$, since each $sigma in H$ fixes $F$.
]

#definition[
  Let $E | F$ be an extension, $G = Gal(E | F)$, and let $K$ be an _intermediate field_, that is, $F subset.eq K subset.eq E$. Put
  $ cal(G)(K) = Gal(E | K) = {sigma in G : sigma(x) = x "for all" x in K}, $
  the subgroup of $G$ fixing $K$ pointwise.
]

#example[
  $CC | RR$ is a Galois extension with $Gal(CC | RR) = {"id", "complex conjugation"} tilde.equiv ZZ slash 2 ZZ$. Indeed, $[CC : RR] = 2$, so $CC | RR$ is normal (@ex-normal), and separable by @lem-sep-deriv, since $"char" RR = 0$. By @prop-gal-order, $|Gal(CC | RR)| = 2$, and complex conjugation is an $RR$-automorphism different from the identity.
]

#theorem[
  Let $E | F$ be a finite Galois extension with $G = Gal(E | F)$.
  + The fixed field of $G$ is $F$.
  + If $H$ is a proper subgroup of $G$, then $cal(F)(H) supset.neq F$.
] <thm-fixed>

#proof[
  1) Let $F_0 = cal(F)(G)$, so $F subset.eq F_0 subset.eq E$. Every $sigma in G$ fixes $F_0$ pointwise, so $G subset.eq Gal(E | F_0)$. Conversely every $F_0$-automorphism of $E$ fixes $F subset.eq F_0$, so $Gal(E | F_0) subset.eq G$, and $G = Gal(E | F_0)$. The extension $E | F_0$ is Galois: it is normal by @cor-normal-top and separable by @thm-sep-tower. By @prop-gal-order, $[E : F_0] = |Gal(E | F_0)| = |G| = [E : F]$. By the tower law $[E : F] = [E : F_0] [F_0 : F]$, so $[F_0 : F] = 1$ and $F_0 = F$.

  // [p2]
  2) Suppose $F = cal(F)(H)$. Since $E | F$ is finite and separable, @thm-prim-elem gives $alpha in E$ with $E = F(alpha)$. Consider
  $ f(x) = product_(sigma in H) (x - sigma(alpha)) in E[x]. $
  We show $f in F[x]$. For $tau in H$ the map $sigma mapsto tau sigma$ is a bijection of $H$, so
  $ product_(sigma in H) (x - tau(sigma(alpha))) = product_(sigma in H) (x - sigma(alpha)). $
  Applying $tau$ to the coefficients of $f$ therefore leaves $f$ unchanged, so every coefficient lies in $cal(F)(H) = F$, and $f in F[x]$. Moreover $f(alpha) = 0$, since $"id" in H$. Then $deg f = |H| < |G| = [E : F]$ by @prop-gal-order. On the other hand $m_(alpha, F)$ divides $f$, so $deg f >= [F(alpha) : F] = [E : F]$, a contradiction. Hence $F != cal(F)(H)$, and since $F subset.eq cal(F)(H)$, $cal(F)(H) supset.neq F$.
]

#remark[
  For a finite Galois extension $E | F$ with $G = Gal(E | F)$ there are two maps between the intermediate fields and the subgroups of $G$:
  $ cal(G): {"intermediate fields"} -> {"subgroups of" G}, quad K mapsto cal(G)(K) = Gal(E | K), $
  $ cal(F): {"subgroups of" G} -> {"intermediate fields"}, quad H mapsto cal(F)(H). $
  By @thm-fundamental-1 they are mutually inverse bijections.
]

#example[
  Let $E = QQ(i, sqrt(5))$ and $F = QQ$. Determine $G = Gal(E | QQ)$, its subgroups and the corresponding intermediate fields.
]

#solution[
  $E$ is the splitting field of $(x^2 + 1)(x^2 - 5)$ over $QQ$, so $E | QQ$ is normal by @thm-normal-split, and separable since $"char" QQ = 0$. We have $[QQ(i) : QQ] = 2$, and $sqrt(5) in.not QQ(i)$: an element $a + b i$ with $a, b in QQ$ is real only if $b = 0$, and $sqrt(5) in.not QQ$. Hence $[E : QQ(i)] = 2$ and $[E : QQ] = 4$, so $|G| = 4$ by @prop-gal-order. Each $sigma in G$ is determined by $sigma(i) in {i, -i}$ and $sigma(sqrt(5)) in {sqrt(5), -sqrt(5)}$, by @thm-perm-roots. These are four possibilities, so all of them occur:
  $ sigma_1: (i, sqrt(5)) mapsto (i, sqrt(5)), & quad sigma_2: (i, sqrt(5)) mapsto (i, -sqrt(5)), \
    sigma_3: (i, sqrt(5)) mapsto (-i, sqrt(5)), & quad sigma_4: (i, sqrt(5)) mapsto (-i, -sqrt(5)). $
  Each $sigma_j$ satisfies $sigma_j^2 = sigma_1 = "id"$. The proper subgroups are ${sigma_1}$, ${sigma_1, sigma_2}$, ${sigma_1, sigma_3}$ and ${sigma_1, sigma_4}$. Since $sigma_2$ fixes $i$, $sigma_3$ fixes $sqrt(5)$, and $sigma_4 (i sqrt(5)) = (-i)(-sqrt(5)) = i sqrt(5)$, each of the three subgroups of order $2$ fixes a field of degree $2$ over $QQ$:
  $ cal(F)({sigma_1, sigma_2}) = QQ(i), quad cal(F)({sigma_1, sigma_3}) = QQ(sqrt(5)), quad cal(F)({sigma_1, sigma_4}) = QQ(i sqrt(5)). $
  Equality holds in each case. For instance $K = cal(F)({sigma_1, sigma_2})$ satisfies $QQ(i) subset.eq K subset.neq E$, since $sigma_2 (sqrt(5)) != sqrt(5)$. As $[E : QQ(i)] = 2$, the tower law forces $K = QQ(i)$. The same argument applies to $QQ(sqrt(5))$ and $QQ(i sqrt(5))$, both of degree $2$ over $QQ$ because $sqrt(5), i sqrt(5) in.not QQ$ are roots of $x^2 - 5$ and $x^2 + 5$. Finally $cal(F)({sigma_1}) = E$ and $cal(F)(G) = QQ$ by @thm-fixed.
  #figure(
    box(width: 7.6cm, height: 3.6cm, {
      let node(x, y, body) = place(dx: x, dy: y, box(width: 0pt, height: 0pt, align(center + horizon, box(fill: white, inset: 2pt, $#body$))))
      let edge(a, b) = place(line(start: a, end: b, stroke: 0.6pt))
      let lab(x, y, body) = place(dx: x, dy: y, box(width: 0pt, height: 0pt, align(center + horizon, text(size: 9pt, $#body$))))
      let E = (0.5cm, 1.8cm)
      let M1 = (3.8cm, 0.4cm)
      let M2 = (3.8cm, 1.8cm)
      let M3 = (3.8cm, 3.2cm)
      let Q = (7.1cm, 1.8cm)
      for M in (M1, M2, M3) {
        edge((E.at(0) + 0.35cm, E.at(1)), (M.at(0) - 0.75cm, M.at(1)))
        edge((M.at(0) + 0.75cm, M.at(1)), (Q.at(0) - 0.35cm, Q.at(1)))
      }
      node(..E, $E$)
      node(..M1, $QQ(sqrt(5))$)
      node(..M2, $QQ(i)$)
      node(..M3, $QQ(i sqrt(5))$)
      node(..Q, $QQ$)
      lab(1.75cm, 0.85cm, $sigma_3$)
      lab(1.75cm, 1.55cm, $sigma_2$)
      lab(1.75cm, 2.75cm, $sigma_4$)
      lab(5.55cm, 0.85cm, 2)
      lab(5.55cm, 1.55cm, 2)
      lab(5.55cm, 2.75cm, 2)
    }),
    caption: [Intermediate fields of $QQ(i, sqrt(5)) | QQ$. An edge from $E$ is labelled with the nontrivial element of $Gal(E | K)$, an edge to $QQ$ with $[K : QQ]$.],
  ) <fig-lattice>
]

#theorem(title: [Fundamental theorem of Galois theory, part I])[
  Let $E | F$ be a finite Galois extension with $G = Gal(E | F)$.
  + The map $cal(F)$ is a bijection from the subgroups of $G$ onto the intermediate fields, with inverse $cal(G)$.
  + Let $H <= G$, $K = cal(F)(H)$ and $sigma in G$. Then $sigma(K) = {sigma(x) : x in K}$ is the intermediate field fixed by $sigma H sigma^(-1)$, that is, $sigma(K) = cal(F)(sigma H sigma^(-1))$.
] <thm-fundamental-1>

#proof[
  1) We show $cal(G)(cal(F)(H)) = H$ for every subgroup $H$ and $cal(F)(cal(G)(K)) = K$ for every intermediate field $K$.

  Let $H <= G$. Every $sigma in H$ fixes $cal(F)(H)$, so $sigma in Gal(E | cal(F)(H)) = cal(G)(cal(F)(H))$, and $H subset.eq cal(G)(cal(F)(H))$. The extension $E | cal(F)(H)$ is Galois, by @cor-normal-top and @thm-sep-tower, with Galois group $cal(G)(cal(F)(H))$. If $H$ were a proper subgroup of it, @thm-fixed 2), applied to $E | cal(F)(H)$, would give that the fixed field of $H$ strictly contains $cal(F)(H)$. But the fixed field of $H$ is $cal(F)(H)$ by definition. Hence $H = cal(G)(cal(F)(H))$.

  Let $K$ be an intermediate field. Then $cal(F)(cal(G)(K))$ is the fixed field of $Gal(E | K)$. Since $E | K$ is Galois, by @cor-normal-top and @thm-sep-tower, this fixed field is $K$ by @thm-fixed 1).

  2) Since $sigma$ is an automorphism of $E$,
  $ cal(F)(sigma H sigma^(-1)) & = {x in E : (sigma tau sigma^(-1))(x) = x "for all" tau in H} \
    & = {x in E : tau(sigma^(-1)(x)) = sigma^(-1)(x) "for all" tau in H} \
    & = {x in E : sigma^(-1)(x) in cal(F)(H)} = sigma(cal(F)(H)) = sigma(K). $
]
